rnings. The repo was not modified (git status clean after the run).\n\n(3) BOTH the report and the extra context missed a third teaching site and a fourth latent defect, in the SAME file the extra context flagged as the brief's step: src/skf-brief-skill/references/gather-intent.md:363 shows the user \"Specific triggers ('use when…', 'reach for this when…')\" as co-equal exemplars, and gather-intent.md:378's headless generic-stub description (\"Use the {skill_name} skill to work with code or content from {target_repo}.\") contains no \"use when\" at all — I fixtured it and it scores 85/100 with the same warning. Separately, src/skf-quick-skill/references/compile.md:38 states the frontmatter description rule with NO trigger-phrase requirement at all, and quick-skill's `skill-check check --fix` call (references/write-and-validate.md:77) is NOT wrapped in the description guard (description-guard-protocol.md:99-100 lists only create-skill validate.md and update-skill write.md as calling workflows) — so for quick-skill the ungrammatical \"Use when <lowercased README text>\" rewrite is not restored and ships.\n\nClaim (a) — \"skill-check only accepts 'Use when'\" — is CONFIRMED BY ME by executing the tool, not merely by reading. The repo does NOT corroborate it anywhere: grepping src/, test/, docs/, tools/ for `use_when_phrase` returns zero hits; the only skill-check description validator SKF documents is `description_field` (the angle-bracket one, compile-assembly-rules.md:19, tessl-dismissal-rules.md:14). compile-assembly-rules.md:18 already knows about the `--fix` prepend behaviour but stops short of concluding that only \"Use when\" satisfies the validator. Claim (b) — that SKF should therefore teach only that form — is a policy call, and per correction (1) I recommend the weaker, non-destructive version of it.",
